WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.080 --> 00:00:03.020
There are several ways to place a higher-voltage segment in a power

2
00:00:03.060 --> 00:00:03.560
architecture.

3
00:00:03.980 --> 00:00:08.100
A higher-voltage supply can feed conversion that produces a lower-voltage rack bus.

4
00:00:08.520 --> 00:00:12.860
Another architecture can distribute higher voltage closer to the equipment before conversion.

5
00:00:13.260 --> 00:00:15.000
Those are different interface contracts.

6
00:00:15.420 --> 00:00:17.360
The animation builds the paths separately.

7
00:00:17.880 --> 00:00:21.300
Follow the voltage label across each segment and stop at the conversion

8
00:00:21.340 --> 00:00:21.700
boundary.

9
00:00:22.180 --> 00:00:26.260
If the equipment still receives fifty volts, its downstream current and power-entry

10
00:00:26.320 --> 00:00:30.940
requirements don't become eight-hundred-volt requirements merely because the upstream supply changed.

11
00:00:31.340 --> 00:00:35.240
If the equipment input does change, compatibility must be established for that

12
00:00:35.300 --> 00:00:36.000
new interface.

13
00:00:36.480 --> 00:00:40.900
The published high-voltage to low-voltage shelf specification is a concrete reference for

14
00:00:40.980 --> 00:00:42.020
one conversion arrangement.

15
00:00:42.360 --> 00:00:45.340
It doesn't establish a universal architecture for every rack.

16
00:00:45.760 --> 00:00:50.500
Keep conversion losses, cooling, protection and service conditions attached to the selected

17
00:00:50.520 --> 00:00:50.940
design.

18
00:00:51.380 --> 00:00:54.920
A boundary map makes these alternatives comparable without blending them into a

19
00:00:55.000 --> 00:00:57.560
single diagram that no actual system implements.

